      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait what do you mean by goblins in my muscles? lol anyway can u finish what u were saying about how they clear it out? how does the body actually get rid of it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "No, no, I'm not talking about actual goblins, just a figure of speech. But, you know, it's a bit like how those goblins might work together to clear out a messy cave. Anyway, back to lactic acid.\n\nSo, after a high-intensity exercise like sprinting, your body needs to get rid of the excess lactic acid. It's a bit like a cleanup crew, and they're made up of various enzymes and transport proteins. One of the main players in this cleanup crew is an enzyme called lactate dehydrogenase (LDH). It's like a little recycling truck that comes in and breaks down the lactic acid into carbon dioxide and water, which can then be exhaled or excreted.\n\nAnother way the body gets rid of lactic acid is through the bloodstream. The lactic acid is transported to the liver, where it's converted back into glucose and released into the bloodstream. It's like a little delivery truck, bringing the lactic acid to the liver and saying, \"Hey, I've got a package here for you.\" And then, the liver sends it off to the rest of the body, where it can be used for energy or excreted.",
